## Identifier Conventions

Requirement IDs use the `{{ID_PREFIX}}-*` namespace.

Current ID forms:

- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-FUN-NNN`: functional requirement.
- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-NFR-NNN`: non-functional requirement.
- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-AC-NNN`: acceptance criterion.

Component meanings:

- `{{ID_PREFIX}}`: project namespace; reduces collision risk and makes search/renumbering practical.
- `FUN` / `NFR`: functional or non-functional requirement.
- `AC`: acceptance criterion — a testable condition proving a requirement is satisfied.
- `NNN`: three-digit, zero-padded sequence number.

Recommended search pattern:

```regex
\b{{ID_PREFIX}}-(FUN|NFR|AC)-[0-9]{3}\b
```

Requirement IDs are internal. They must never appear in user-facing text (CLI help, error messages, README, USAGE) — only in docstrings, comments, tests, commit messages, and `docs/`.

### External / Team IDs

If a broader team maintains separate requirement numbers or ticket IDs, do not force this project's IDs to match prematurely. Maintain a mapping table (workspace ID, external ID, source system, relationship type, confidence, notes) instead of renumbering.

